@startuml deployment
!include _common.iuml
title Enterprise Commerce Platform — Production Deployment (Docker Compose on two VMs)

actor "Customer · Guest\n(browser)" as Shopper
actor "Staff · Warehouse\nSupport · Admin" as Operator

node "VM  app-01\nDocker Compose" as APP {
  component "nginx\nTLS · reverse proxy · static\nrate-limit backstop" as NGINX
  node "ecp-web   {1..N}" as WEB {
    artifact "Next.js standalone\nApp Router · RSC" as WEBART
  }
  node "ecp-api   {1..N}" as API {
    artifact "ecp-app.jar\nSpring Boot 4.1.1 · Java 21\n13 Modulith modules" as APIART
    component "Outbox relay\n(in-process)" as RELAY
    component "Scheduler\n(profile: scheduler)" as SCHED
  }
}

node "VM  data-01\nDocker Compose" as DATA {
  database "PostgreSQL\ntransactional source of truth\n+ outbox table" as PG
  database "Redis\ncache-aside · hot data\nrate limit · flash-sale pre-filter" as REDIS
  queue "Kafka  (KRaft)\nevent backbone" as KAFKA
  database "Elasticsearch\nCatalog search read model" as ES
  database "MongoDB\nreporting · flexible read models" as MONGO
}

cloud "External providers" as EXT {
  component "Payment\nGateway" as PAYGW
  component "Shipping\nCarrier" as CARRIER
  component "Email Service\nProvider" as ESP
}

Shopper --> NGINX : HTTPS 443
Operator --> NGINX : HTTPS 443

NGINX --> WEB : HTTP 3000\nnetwork: app
NGINX --> API : HTTP 8080  /api/v1\nnetwork: app
WEB --> NGINX : server-side fetch\n/api/v1

API --> PG : JDBC 5432
API --> REDIS : RESP 6379
API --> ES : HTTP 9200
API --> MONGO : 27017
API --> KAFKA : consume 9092
RELAY --> PG : poll unpublished
RELAY --> KAFKA : publish 9092

API --> PAYGW : authorise · capture · refund
API --> CARRIER : dispatch · track
API --> ESP : send

PAYGW ..> NGINX : async settlement webhook
CARRIER ..> NGINX : async tracking webhook

note bottom of DATA
  Single failure domain. data-01 has no replica, so
  NFR-AVAIL-01 (99.9% monthly) rests on backup/restore
  and VM-level recovery, not on redundancy.
  Recorded as a Negative consequence in ADR-0028.
end note

note bottom of API
  The Scheduler is a first-class trigger, not a human
  caller (P5) -- but it must fire once, not once per
  replica. Only one ecp-api runs the `scheduler` profile.
  See ADR-0028 and Deployment Diagram section 6.
end note

note right of NGINX
  All six roles enter through one REST API.
  Authorisation is applied server-side at the
  application boundary, never by a client. (P16)
end note
@enduml
